ap private insurance rates  which they proceeded to do which caused  a big exodus from Cape Cod Martha's  Vineyard Nantucket of the insurers so  within a year or two most property  owners that this place has got notices  from their insurance companies that  their policies wouldn't be renewed so  then there was another uproar which  resulted very similar to Florida in the  state coming in with a its own plan  ironically called the fair plan which is  a massive subsidization of  quite wealthy people on the beach by  less wealthy people inland and so the  root of the problem and I agree with the  senator others and I talked to a lot of  insurance people in Massachusetts is  that we have a lot of people inland  subsidizing risk on the coast and if we  keep doing that through all the means  we've talked about we're gonna keep  having a problem and I think it takes  political courage to say as I think you  said for the National Flood Insurance  Program that premiums ought to reflect  risk people want to build in a dangerous  base okay it's a free country but don't  make everyone else pay for that risk so  let me make one significant caveat to  that because I think it's and and I  started this conversation so I'm  conditioning myself here I think what  we're talking about right is these big  houses that sit literally on the  shoreline but as flood maps move further  inland and and frankly as you just look  at the experience of flooding in some of  our coastal cities like Stanford and  Bridgeport we're gonna be dealing with  flood prone areas that include a lot of  very low-income neighborhoods as well  and so one of the caveats here and  things to be careful about is that  should you shift a lot of the risk onto  the homeowners or the landlord's you're  gonna get a lot of those three million  dollar homes on the shoreline but you  may also get a lot of low-income  properties in Bridgeport and Stanford in  Norwalk as well the fact is we have an  enormous ly high percentage 